# OCI Active Data Guard — {% if mode == 'create' %}create standby and associate{% else %}check existing database pair{% endif %}

{% if mode == 'create' %}
Creates a new standby database from an existing primary database on an existing
{% if creation_type == 'ExistingVmCluster' %}VM cluster (Exadata).{% else %}bare metal or Exadata DB system. ExistingDbSystem is not the target option for Base Database VM systems.{% endif %}
This does not reuse or overwrite an independently created peer database.
Active Data Guard defaults to enabled; verify your license entitlement.
The supported combination here is MAXIMUM_PERFORMANCE with ASYNC transport.

## Before applying

- Provision the primary database and target infrastructure first. Check OCI service
  support, compatible database versions, capacity, and IAM permissions in both regions.
- Establish Data Guard network connectivity first, including required listener ports,
  routing, DNS and security rules. The networking directories generated by this app
  have separate state: apply and verify them before this directory.
- Use the primary database's region for `oci_region`. A target in another region
  must satisfy OCI's cross-region service prerequisites.
- Optionally set `peer_db_home_id` to use a compatible existing target DB Home.
  Otherwise OCI creates a DB Home. Optional unique name/SID can be left blank.
- Supply the same admin password as the primary database when Terraform prompts.
  It is intentionally absent from generated files and saved app configuration.

## Destruction and state

The provider requires `delete_standby_db_home_on_delete = true`: destroying this
association terminates the standby DB Home/DB system. `prevent_destroy = true`
blocks planned deletion and replacement while the resource block remains in configuration.
Removing the entire block also removes that guard. Review any lifecycle change with your DBA,
especially when an existing DB Home is shared. Resource import is not supported.
Terraform state and saved plans can contain the database password even though the
variable is sensitive. Use a secured remote state backend and restrict access.
{% else %}
Read-only lookup using the two existing database OCIDs. This lists association API
records on the reporting primary database and selects the specified peer.
It shows the association ID, roles, state, redo apply lag/rate and Active Data Guard flag.
It does not create or change a database, associate two independent databases, or
turn on Active Data Guard. If no association exists, the output precondition fails
with guidance instead of claiming success. No database password is required.

The OCI association resource cannot attach an independently created standby and
cannot import an existing association. To create a managed standby, use the
create-standby option. Ask your DBA about independently configured databases.
Configurations using the newer Data Guard Group API are outside this association lookup.
{% endif %}

## Run

Configure OCI provider authentication (for example `~/.oci/config` and its private
key), review `terraform.tfvars`, and run from this directory:

```sh
terraform init
terraform fmt
terraform validate
terraform plan
terraform apply
terraform output
```

`validate` checks configuration only; `plan` and `apply` contact OCI.
For OpenTofu, replace `terraform` with `tofu`.

[Association resource](https://docs.oracle.com/en-us/iaas/tools/terraform-provider-oci/latest/docs/r/database_data_guard_association.html)
and [existing association lookup](https://docs.oracle.com/en-us/iaas/tools/terraform-provider-oci/latest/docs/d/database_data_guard_associations.html).
